# Collatz, in unary. n is written as n ones between the end markers:
#
#   rw progs/collatz.rw '<1111111.'     ->  1.
#
# Each iteration walks a single marker across the tape; there is never more
# than one marker alive, which is what keeps the rule order unambiguous.
#
#   P/Q  parity walk, eating 1s into as and toggling even/odd
#   H    halving walk (even): pairs of as become bs
#   G    return walk, turning the bs back into 1s
#   T    tripling walk (odd): each a becomes 111, then <T adds the +1

# reached 1: say so and stop
<P1.->.1.

# first move only: no bare 1 ever sits next to < again until we are done
<1-><P1

# parity: P means even so far, Q means odd so far
P1->aQ
Q1->aP

# even -- carry the marker back to the left, then halve
P.->H.
aH->Ha
Haa->bH
H.->G.
bG->G1
<G-><P

# odd -- 3n+1, right to left, with the +1 spent on restarting the walk
Q.->T.
aT->T111
<T-><P1
